 this  one has almost like rogue elements to it  where you have a city map and you're  choosing which level you want to go to  someone might be more suburban one might  be like a train one might be more like a  skyscraper kind of setting and you're  not locked to the individual screens  like you were in the first one you can  go up down any direction  the aliens are way more mobile this time  you have a double jump you can dive  dodge roll you have iframes on those  moves so that you're actually like you  can weave in and out of the chaos and  that's probably the most exciting thing  about Alien Hominid still is the chaos  that's on screen by the way also loot  new for this game you got hats you got  guns with all sorts of special abilities  you've got passive abilities so a lot of  differences from the first game in this  sequel / reimagining clash whatever they  want to call it I just call it Alien  Hominid invasion  [Music]  hear me out  undertale meets persona in an indie RPG  that's she dreams elsewhere now if you  know me that's speaking my language  having played it you kind of get the  undertale vibes from the retro style  from the perspective of when you're in  the overworld and especially when you're  in the perspective of combat because it  has that earthbound style but it draws a  lot of inspiration from persona when it  comes to the combat because of hitting  weaknesses and having different party  members with different skills now those  are the gameplay elements that hook me  but I think this game is more important  because it's coming from an independent  developer who's collabing with other  people but it's essentially a solo  project for developer Davion Goodin I  see a lot of the passion that's brought  into his game because he's writing it  and a lot of the dialogue a lot of the  character interactions come from an  honest place and I think it has a  creative freedom to create characters  that are more reflective of himself and  the people that he knows and that's kind  of what's shining through so I'm really  looking forward to she dreams elsewhere  it's speaking my language it's inspired  by the games that I love and I can't  wait to see what it is when it finally  launches  [Music]  my favorite game that I saw at PAX East  was Magic legends it looks a lot like  Diablo it plays a lot like Diablo and if  you have seen the Diablo before play  detail before you might see an isometric  action RPG and think I've seen this  before I've played just before the deck  building aspects really had a whole new  wrinkle to it rather than cycling  through cooldowns are actually cycling  through a deck of 12 cards the build  they had here was pretty overpowered you  can kind of hit any button and you do  something cool but you could tell just  by the little taste that I played that  eventually are gonna get to know your  deck well enough just like a real deck  builder where you're gonna know what the  powers are you gonna know what the icons  are you can read the screen instantly  and know when you want to trigger  certain spells it gives it a really cool  look and feel they had shown two classes  before the mind mage and the geomancer  those were basically an aggro and a sort  of back off hit from a distance the cool  new thing that showed here was called  the beasts collar which is basically a  summoner any class can summon beasts but  the beasts collar can summon a lot of  them a lot of their cards are built  around summoning beasts so I was just  rolling deep with like three or four big  big creatures at my back and we were  just taking down monsters like nothing  I've never gotten into the magic lore  but this game might actually finally  convince me  my favorite game I saw is recompile  which is a self-described Metroid  inspired game with a Tron aesthetic you  play as a computer program who's in a  hostile mainframe like I said the whole  thing it looks exactly like Tron Legacy  which is just like aesthetically one of  my favorite things ever so already  pretty on board with that the Metroid  inspired elements come in sort of the  way your character progresses at the  start you're just gaining simple  platforming abilities you can jump you  can double jump but then you get a gun  and it becomes a third-person shooter  and then you get the ability to hack  which allows you to freeze time and  manipulate objects you can move boxes  around and stack them which you can use  to solve different puzzles and then you  can also hack enemies and you can like  make them fight for you and that's where  the game just starts like escalating in  terms of like how your character like  interacts with the world eventually  you're gaining the ability to do like  the Metro like screwattack where you can  like infinitely jump and infinitely -  you get a glide ability that just lets  you fly anywhere in the world so there's  a huge level of freedom and how like you  interact with the world where you just  start very basic and by the end you're  just you can you explore this world in  any way you want  [Music]  so those are the games we were looking  forward to most from PAX East 2020 and  for more from the show you can check out  all the gameplay we've captured on our  YouTube pages that you can want our  impressions of everything we've played  and go to GameSpot calm and check out  our PAX East gallery thanks for watching  [Music]
 

